  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe will use a multi-resolution optimization strategy with adaptive smoothing to transform the non-convex problem into a sequence of convex approximations. The central idea is to:\n- Smooth the max operation using a smooth max function\n- Introduce adaptive penalty weighting based on local geometry\n- Combine multi-start optimization with gradient-enhanced local search\n- Use dimensionality reduction via Fourier projection\n  \nThis approach addresses:\n- The high-dimensionality issue (hundreds of free variables)\n- The challenging max operation in the objective\n- Local optimum trapping by using multiple resolutions\n- Computational expense by solving smaller, easier problems first\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Variables: Piecewise constant h_i via samples from a trigonometric polynomial basis\n- Objective:Minimize max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints:0\u2264h_i\u22641, \u2211h_i=1\n- Smoothed objective:Use log-sum-exp for continuous formulation\n- Grid-based projection:Use linear programming to maintain feasibility\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Initialization\n    1. If initial_h_values, project to nearest grid size\n    2. Else generate Pareto-distributed initial ensemble\n\n   b. Multi-resolution optimization\n    1. Apply fine-scale filter (Fourier projection)\n    2. Compute directional derivatives\n    3. Local search with smoothing parameter schedule\n    4. Geometric mean penalty to maintain feasibility\n\n   c. Core update rule\n    while time_remaining > 0:\n        t = current_time()\n        grid = finest_feasible_grid(t)\n        residual = compute_feasibility_vector(h, grid)\n        grad = project_gradient(h, grid, target_penalty)\n        h_new = step_size * (h - grad) + random_fluctuation\n        h_new = projection_operator(h_new, constraint_type='waterfill')\n        accept_prob = arma_max(residual, grad_squared, sigma)\n        if accept_prob >= uniform(0,1):\n          h = h_new\n          record_improvement(grid)\n        time_remaining = deadline - current_time()\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Four grid scales:32,64,128,256 points\n- Smoothing parameters: [10,50,100] to target different lags\n- Multi-start:Use different seeds for basis vectors\n- Adaptive penalty:Dynamic balancing between feasibility and objective\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Time allocation:70% grid descent,30% local search\n- Fallback:Return interpolated finest grid solution\n- Local optimum escape:Periodic multi-start grids\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Check normalized:0\u2264h_i\u22641 and \u222bh=0.5\n   - Recompute C5 with true correlation\n   - Verify against lower-dimensional approximation\n   - Return only h where C5 improved by >5% over initial",
